    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The innate spinal cord injury (SCI) repair mechanisms in humans are incapable of regenerating functional motor neurons. Cellular transplantation of neural progenitor cells (NPCs) has been suggested as a potential solution to this issue. Robust populations of NPCs cells may be derived from human induced pluripotent stem cells (hiPSCs) and can restore communication along injured axons by establishing relay grafts. However, developing efficient cell-delivery and engraftment mechanisms for transplantation remains a significant challenge. Previous research has shown that cell delivery within carrier gels improves cellular viability upon transplantation, but a majority of these gels are difficult to synthesize and incompatible with a diversity of cell types. A recently developed family of hydrogels (MITCH-PEG and SHIELD) may offer an attractive solution for enhancing transplantation in SCI repair.  Not only are these biocompatible hydrogel materials capable of encapsulating hiPSC-NPCs at physiological conditions, they are also both reproducible and customizable in nature. Examining the behavior of hiPSC-NPCs encapsulated in the hydrogels demonstrated that the hydrogels were capable of protecting and supporting the hiPSC-NPCs. These results suggest that encapsulating hiPSC-NPCs in a hydrogel prior to transplantation may serve as an effective strategy for increasing functional regeneration in SCI repair. In the future, technologies like MITCH-PEG and SHIELD will improve transplantation techniques by serving as a platform for a variety of cell types and therapies, particularly in the central nervous system where regeneration is not generally common.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
